{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 15, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "the social network (2010) is a razor-sharp dissection of ambition, betrayal, and the birth of an empire, wrapped in a sleek, dialogue-driven package that crackles with wit and venom. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the film is a fictionalized account of mark zuckerberg’s creation of facebook, though it’s less a biography and more a character study of genius, hubris, and the cost of revolutionizing the world.\n\nthe performances are electric, led by jesse eisenberg’s portrayal of zuckerberg—a socially inept, driven, and often insufferable prodigy whose genius is matched only by his emotional detachment. andrew garfield as eduardo severin delivers the film’s heart, a loyal friend whose downfall is as tragic as it is inevitable. justin timberlake’s sean parker is a scene-stealer, a silver-tongued svengali whose charm masks a ruthless opportunism. armie hammer as cameron and tyler winklevoss is a standout, using physicality and timing to ground the film’s more absurd elements.\n\nfincher’s direction is clinical, favoring cold blues and sterile whites that mirror the emotional frostiness of the characters. the editing—by kirk baxter and angus wall—is nothing short of masterful, with rapid-fire dialogue scenes that feel like verbal chess matches. trent reznor and atticus ross’s score is a haunting, pulsing backdrop that elevates the tension to almost unbearable levels.\n\nthe script is a triumph of sorkin’s signature style: fast, dense, and endlessly quotable. lines like \"you’re going to go through life thinking girls don’t like you because you’re a nerd… it’ll never occur to you that it might be because you’re an asshole\" and \"i’m not a bad guy. i’m a very good guy who’s fucked by people who are supposed to be my friends\" are delivered with surgical precision, each word a dagger.\n\nthe film’s greatest strength is its refusal to glorify its subjects. zuckerberg isn’t a hero, nor is he a villain—he’s a flawed human whose creation both connects and isolates, whose ambition both builds and destroys. the supporting cast, including brenda song as zuckerberg’s long-suffering girlfriend, alison brie as a california girl with a hidden edge, and max minghella as a harvard entrepreneur, all add layers to the film’s exploration of loyalty, class, and the price of success.\n\nvisually, the film is a marvel. fincher’s use of shallow focus, tight close-ups, and precise framing keeps the viewer off-balance, mirroring the characters’ emotional detachment. the courtroom scenes, though fictionalized, are particularly gripping, with the back-and-forth between lawyers and witnesses feeling like a high-stakes poker game.\n\nif there’s a flaw, it’s that the film occasionally romanticizes the early days of facebook—a movement that, in reality, had far darker consequences. but this is a minor quibble in a film that otherwise balances its ambition with biting cynicism.\n\nin the end, the social network is more than just a movie about facebook. it’s a meditation on the loneliness of genius, the fragility of trust, and the birth of an idea that would change society forever. it’s a film that demands to be watched, rewatched, and quoted endlessly. a modern classic.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.37151509523391724}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.0030423568096011877}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " (", "logprob": -1.2494654655456543}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.0011461363174021244}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -5.125986263010418e-06}, {"token": ")", "logprob": -0.5761526823043823}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.18841396272182465}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.017689086496829987}, {"token": " razor", "logprob": -0.9233142137527466}, {"token": "-sh", 